  "people": {
    "josef-kastl-1840": {
      "name": "Josef Kastl",
      "sex": "M",
      "branch": "josef",
      "summary": "Came to America in 1868",
      "chapter": "s043",
      "facts": [
        {
          "type": "name",
          "value": "Joseph Kastl",
          "sources": [
            {
              "scan": "s043",
              "quote": "Josef Kastl was born 15 January 1840 in Vsepadly, Bohemia, house No. 11"
            },
            {
              "scan": "s010",
              "quote": "Joseph came to America April 22, 1868."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "birth",
          "value": "15 January 1840, Všepadly, house No. 11, Bohemia",
          "sources": [
            {
              "scan": "s043",
              "quote": "Josef Kastl was born 15 January 1840 in Vsepadly, Bohemia, house No. 11"
            },
            {
              "scan": "s016",
              "quote": "Josef 1840-1921"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "marriage",
          "value": "Dorota Blaha, 1863, Bohemia",
          "sources": [
            {
              "scan": "s043",
              "quote": "Josef Kastl married Dorota Blaha in 1863 in Bohemia."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "immigration",
          "value": "Sailed from Bremen on the S.S. Berlin with his wife and two children; arrived at Baltimore 22 April 1868",
          "sources": [
            {
              "scan": "s043",
              "quote": "Josef and Dorota sailed from Bremen, Germany on the ship S.S. Berlin, arriving at the Port of Baltimore on 22 April 1868, with two children, Mathias 3, and Theresia 9 months."
            },
            {
              "scan": "s009",
              "quote": "Following Wenzel was Joseph, wife Dorothea and two children Mathias and Theresia, arriving April 22, 1868, on the ship SS Berlin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "occupation",
          "value": "Stone mason in Omaha; worked for the Union Pacific Railroad building station houses through Nebraska and Wyoming",
          "sources": [
            {
              "scan": "s071",
              "quote": "Josef first came to Omaha where he was a stone mason. To raise money for his family, he worked for the Union Pacific Railroad building station houses through Nebraska and Wyoming."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "homestead",
          "value": "Filed 20 June 1868 at Nebraska City, application No. 2069: E½ of SE¼, Section 4, Township 14 North, 80 acres, Newman Precinct, Saunders County",
          "sources": [
            {
              "scan": "s010",
              "quote": "On June 20, 1868, he filed for homestead at the land office in Nebraska City. Application number was 2069. Description of the land was E½ of the SE¼. Section 4, Township 14 North, 80 acres in Newman Precinct."
            },
            {
              "scan": "s071",
              "quote": "went to homestead west of Omaha, in Newman Precinct, Saunders County."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "death",
          "value": "1921",
          "sources": [
            {
              "scan": "s043",
              "quote": "Josef died in 1921 and was buried at Brainard, Nebraska."
            },
            {
              "scan": "s016",
              "quote": "Josef 1840-1921"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "burial",
          "value": "Brainard, Nebraska",
          "sources": [
            {
              "scan": "s043",
              "quote": "was buried at Brainard, Nebraska."
            },
            {
              "scan": "s009",
              "quote": "Joseph was buried at Brainard, Nebraska"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "scan": "s071",
          "text": "When he returned to Omaha, he equipped a wagon pulled by a tean {sic} of oxen, and went to homestead west of Omaha, in Newman Precinct, Saunders County.\n\nHe proceeded to build a log cabin, but in the meantime lived with his wife in a nearby cave dug into the side of a hill.\n\nA missionary priest on horseback would periodically visit Josef's cabin and say Mass, using a table that is still in the family."
        },
        {
          "scan": "s010",
          "text": "July 6, 1875, in his final proof required under the homestead act, Joseph states he built a house of logs, 16x32, with two doors and four windows. The remains of this log house are still visible on the original site."
        },
        {
          "scan": "s011",
          "text": "\"The Kastl house, like many other Czech log houses that we have recorded, was one of the finest examples of the art of log building in the State of Nebraska. The Czechs were quite skilled in these techniques and are noted for their ability to build tight, chinkless walls (little or no space between logs), and finely — crafted corner notches. The Kastl house notches, while almost \"square\", are really a \"ful-dovetail\" notch, the most difficult notch to craft of any built in the United States."
        },
        {
          "scan": "s012",
          "text": "The families of Wenzel and Joseph lived in Omaha while the men were erecting their log houses. It was during this time that Joseph's children, Matthias and Theresa, both died, and were buried in Omaha."
        },
        {
          "scan": "s014",
          "text": "A trip to Nebraska City by Peter and Joseph was made in 1870. They had raised some corn, a little more than they thought they needed. They decided to market the corn in Nebraska City where they would receive the highest price. The distance traveled would be about one hundred miles each way. There were two wagon loads of corn, thirty bushels in each wagon, drawn by oxen. When they arrived, they unloaded the corn, and sat in a small building waiting for their money. They opened the door and saw their oxen headed down the road a quarter of a mile away. Someone had started the oxen with the idea of fleecing the settlers. After catching up with the oxen, Joseph, being outspoken and short tempered, took matters in his own hands. He went back with his black snake whip and shotgun, and in strong broken English demanded the money for the corn. He was quickly paid and Peter and Joseph were on their way home."
        }
      ],
      "photos": [
        "s043-ph1",
        "s010-ph1",
        "s011-ph1"
      ]
    },
